window.QUIZ_BANK['junit'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['junit']||[]).concat([
{id:4921,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @ExtendWith in JUnit 5?',options:['Specifies the Java version for the test','Registers one or more extensions that add behavior to the test lifecycle, such as setup/teardown, parameter resolution, or condition evaluation','Extends the test class with additional assertion methods','Configures the Maven Surefire plugin'],answer:1,explanation:'@ExtendWith(SomeExtension.class) registers a JUnit 5 extension on a test class or method. Extensions implement one or more Extension lifecycle interfaces to hook into test execution. Multiple extensions can be registered and they are applied in declaration order.'},
{id:4922,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which interface does an extension implement to run code before all tests in a container?',options:['BeforeTestCallback','BeforeAllCallback','PreTestCallback','TestSetupCallback'],answer:1,explanation:'BeforeAllCallback (org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.BeforeAllCallback) has a single method beforeAll(ExtensionContext context) that runs once before all tests in the container. It is the extension equivalent of @BeforeAll, but applied from outside the test class.'},
{id:4923,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which interface does an extension implement to run code after all tests in a container?',options:['PostTestCallback','AfterTestCallback','AfterAllCallback','TearDownCallback'],answer:2,explanation:'AfterAllCallback (afterAll(ExtensionContext)) executes once after all tests in the container complete. Like BeforeAllCallback, it is invoked by the JUnit Platform and does not require modifying the test class itself.'},
{id:4924,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which extension interface is used to run logic before each individual test method?',options:['BeforeEachCallback','PreEachCallback','TestSetupCallback','EachTestCallback'],answer:0,explanation:'BeforeEachCallback (beforeEach(ExtensionContext)) is called before each test method. It is the extension equivalent of @BeforeEach and allows shared setup logic to be extracted into a reusable extension rather than repeating it in every test class.'},
{id:4925,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which extension interface is used to run logic after each individual test method?',options:['AfterEachCallback','PostEachCallback','TestCleanupCallback','TearEachCallback'],answer:0,explanation:'AfterEachCallback (afterEach(ExtensionContext)) is called after each test method. Useful for cleanup like closing connections or resetting state. When combined with BeforeEachCallback in the same extension, it provides complete per-test lifecycle management.'},
{id:4926,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the TestExecutionExceptionHandler extension interface do?',options:['Catches assertion errors and converts them to warnings','Intercepts exceptions thrown during test execution and can rethrow, swallow, or replace them','Handles timeout exceptions only','Handles exceptions in @BeforeAll methods'],answer:1,explanation:'TestExecutionExceptionHandler.handleTestExecutionException(ExtensionContext, Throwable) is called when a test throws an exception. The extension can rethrow the exception (test fails), swallow it (test passes), or throw a different exception, enabling retry logic or expected-exception handling.'},
{id:4927,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the ParameterResolver extension interface do in JUnit 5?',options:['Validates method parameters at compile time','Provides dependency injection for test method and lifecycle method parameters by resolving them at runtime','Converts parameter types for @ParameterizedTest','Maps parameter names to CSV columns'],answer:1,explanation:'ParameterResolver has two methods: supportsParameter(ParameterContext, ExtensionContext) determines if the resolver handles a parameter, and resolveParameter(ParameterContext, ExtensionContext) returns the value to inject. It is the foundation for @TempDir injection and custom dependency injection frameworks.'},
{id:4928,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @RegisterExtension and how does it differ from @ExtendWith?',options:['@RegisterExtension is a class-level annotation; @ExtendWith is method-level','@RegisterExtension is a field annotation that registers an extension instance programmatically, allowing constructor parameters and state; @ExtendWith uses a class reference only','They are identical','@RegisterExtension is deprecated; use @ExtendWith instead'],answer:1,explanation:'@RegisterExtension on a field registers the extension using an already-created instance. This allows passing constructor arguments (e.g., port numbers, connection strings) that cannot be provided via @ExtendWith(class). Static fields create class-scoped extensions; instance fields create method-scoped extensions.'},
{id:4929,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How is an extension auto-registered globally in JUnit 5 without annotations?',options:['Place the extension in the default package','Register it via ServiceLoader: create META-INF/services/org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension with the fully qualified class name, and enable junit.jupiter.extensions.autodetection.enabled=true','Annotate it with @AutoExtension','Put it in a package named extensions'],answer:1,explanation:'Set junit.jupiter.extensions.autodetection.enabled=true in junit-platform.properties and register the extension via Java ServiceLoader (META-INF/services/org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension). JUnit discovers and applies it to all test classes automatically, without any per-class annotation.'},
{id:4930,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledOnOs do in JUnit 5?',options:['Sets the OS for the CI environment','Executes the test only if the current OS matches one of the specified OS values (e.g., WINDOWS, LINUX, MAC)','Enables OS-specific assertion modes','Configures OS-level file permissions for temp directories'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledOnOs({OS.WINDOWS, OS.LINUX}) causes the test to run only on the specified operating systems. On non-matching platforms, the test is disabled (aborted). It is a conditional execution annotation from the JUnit Jupiter Conditions extension.'},
{id:4931,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledOnOs do in JUnit 5?',options:['Disables the entire test class on all OSes','Prevents the test from running on specified operating systems','Disables OS detection','Marks the test as OS-independent'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledOnOs(OS.WINDOWS) skips the test on Windows. It is the inverse of @EnabledOnOs, useful for excluding tests on known incompatible platforms without disabling them elsewhere. Multiple OS values can be specified.'},
{id:4932,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledOnJre do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables Java runtime-level logging','Runs the test only on specified JRE versions (e.g., JRE.JAVA_11, JRE.JAVA_17)','Sets the JRE compiler target version','Enables JRE-specific assertion modes'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_17) runs the test only when executing on Java 17. JRE constants include JAVA_8 through JAVA_21+ depending on the JUnit version. This is useful for testing JRE version-specific behavior.'},
{id:4933,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledIfSystemProperty do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables the test if a system property file exists','Enables the test if a specified JVM system property matches a given regex value','Sets a system property for the test','Reads a system property as a test parameter'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIfSystemProperty(named="env", matches="ci") enables the test only if the JVM system property "env" matches the regex "ci". If the property is absent or does not match, the test is disabled. Multiple conditions can be combined by stacking annotations.'},
{id:4934,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable do in JUnit 5?',options:['Reads environment variable values as test inputs','Enables the test only if the specified environment variable exists and its value matches the given regex','Sets an environment variable before the test','Disables all tests unless the variable is set'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named="RUN_INTEGRATION_TESTS", matches="true") runs the test only when that environment variable equals "true" (regex match). This is common for separating integration tests from unit tests in CI pipelines.'},
{id:4935,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @EnabledIf with a SpEL expression do in JUnit 5?',options:['Evaluates a Java boolean expression at compile time','Evaluates a Spring Expression Language (SpEL) expression at runtime; the test is enabled if the expression evaluates to true','Enables the test if a Spring context is available','Evaluates a JavaScript expression in GraalVM'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIf("#{systemProperties[\'os.name\'].contains(\'Linux\')}") evaluates a SpEL expression to determine if the test runs. This is provided by spring-test integration and allows rich conditional logic using Spring\'s expression language. Without Spring, @EnabledIfSystemProperty or @EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable are alternatives.'},
{id:4936,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you use Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['Annotate the test class with @RunWith(MockitoJUnitRunner.class)','Add @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) to the test class and use @Mock, @InjectMocks, @Spy, @Captor annotations','Call MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this) in @BeforeEach','Use @MockBean from Spring Boot Test'],answer:1,explanation:'With the mockito-junit-jupiter artifact, add @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) to the test class. Then use @Mock to create mocks, @InjectMocks to inject them, @Spy for partial mocks, and @Captor for argument captors. The extension handles initialization and validation automatically.'},
{id:4937,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Mock do when used with MockitoExtension?',options:['Creates a real instance of the annotated class','Creates a Mockito mock of the annotated field\'s type and injects it into the test class','Marks the field as a stub','Validates that the field is not null'],answer:1,explanation:'@Mock creates a Mockito mock object for the annotated field. MockitoExtension initializes it before each test. The mock records all method calls and returns default values (null, 0, false) unless stubbed with when().thenReturn(). Mockito verifies no unexpected interactions if strict stubbing is enabled.'},
{id:4938,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @InjectMocks do when used with MockitoExtension?',options:['Injects Spring beans into the test class','Creates an instance of the annotated class and injects @Mock and @Spy fields using constructor, setter, or field injection','Marks the class under test for reflection-based injection','Proxies all method calls through Mockito'],answer:1,explanation:'@InjectMocks creates an instance of the annotated class (the system under test) and injects all @Mock and @Spy fields from the test class into it using Mockito\'s injection strategies: constructor injection first, then setter, then field injection. This avoids manual wiring.'},
{id:4939,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Spy do in Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['Creates a full mock with no real method calls','Creates a partial mock that wraps a real object, calling real methods unless specifically stubbed','Monitors method calls without stubbing','Creates a read-only proxy of the object'],answer:1,explanation:'@Spy wraps a real instance of the class. Non-stubbed methods execute the actual implementation. Only specifically stubbed methods return mock values. This is useful when most behavior should be real but a specific method needs isolation (e.g., an external call).'},
{id:4940,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Captor do in Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['Captures test output for assertions','Creates an ArgumentCaptor for the annotated field\'s type, used to capture arguments passed to mock methods for later assertion','Records all mock interactions for replay','Captures the return value of stubbed methods'],answer:1,explanation:'@Captor creates an ArgumentCaptor<T> initialized by MockitoExtension. After a mock method is invoked, captor.getValue() returns the captured argument. This is useful for asserting the exact argument passed to a mock when the argument is a complex object created inside the system under test.'},
{id:4941,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpringBootTest do in JUnit 5?',options:['Starts a minimal Spring context with no beans','Loads the full Spring application context for integration testing; can start an embedded web server depending on the webEnvironment attribute','Runs the test without Spring context','Replaces @ContextConfiguration in all cases'],answer:1,explanation:'@SpringBootTest loads the complete Spring ApplicationContext defined by @SpringBootApplication, enabling end-to-end integration tests. The webEnvironment attribute controls whether a real server is started (RANDOM_PORT, DEFINED_PORT), a mock server (MOCK), or no web environment (NONE).'},
{id:4942,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @WebMvcTest do in Spring Boot test?',options:['Loads the full application context including service and repository layers','Loads only the web layer (Controllers, ControllerAdvice, Filters, etc.) and configures MockMvc; service beans must be mocked','Tests WebSocket endpoints only','Runs tests against a real HTTP server'],answer:1,explanation:'@WebMvcTest(UserController.class) creates a Spring context with only MVC-related beans. It auto-configures MockMvc. Services, repositories, and other beans are not loaded; they must be provided as @MockBean. This gives fast, focused controller tests.'},
{id:4943,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DataJpaTest do in Spring Boot testing?',options:['Loads the full Spring Boot context for JPA testing','Configures an in-memory database, scans for @Entity and JPA repositories, and runs tests in a rolled-back transaction by default','Loads only controller beans','Tests REST APIs with JPA entities'],answer:1,explanation:'@DataJpaTest sets up a minimal Spring context for JPA: configures H2 (or another embedded DB), discovers @Entity classes and Spring Data repositories, and wraps each test in a transaction that is rolled back after the test. Non-JPA beans must be provided separately.'},
{id:4944,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @MockBean do in Spring Boot test?',options:['Creates a Mockito mock and adds it to the Spring ApplicationContext as a bean, replacing any existing bean of the same type','Creates a mock but does not register it in the context','Is identical to @Mock from Mockito','Only works with @WebMvcTest'],answer:0,explanation:'@MockBean creates a Mockito mock and registers it in the Spring context, replacing any existing bean of that type. This is the key difference from @Mock, which only creates a mock in the test class without touching the context. Reset occurs between each test by default.'},
{id:4945,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpyBean do in Spring Boot test?',options:['Creates a new partial mock independently of the Spring context','Wraps an existing Spring bean with a Mockito spy, allowing real method calls with selective stubbing within the Spring context','Marks a bean as read-only in the context','Creates a spy that automatically stubs all methods'],answer:1,explanation:'@SpyBean wraps the actual Spring-managed bean in a Mockito spy. Unlike @MockBean, the real implementation is invoked for non-stubbed methods. Useful when you need to verify interactions with a real bean or stub only some methods.'},
{id:4946,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @TestConfiguration do in Spring Boot?',options:['Replaces the main application configuration entirely','Defines additional beans or overrides existing beans specifically for tests; does not replace @SpringBootApplication scanning','Sets the test profile for Spring','Configures the test database connection'],answer:1,explanation:'@TestConfiguration marks a class as providing additional bean definitions or overrides for a specific test. Unlike @Configuration, @TestConfiguration beans are not picked up during normal component scanning; they must be explicitly imported or declared inside the test class.'},
{id:4947,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is MockMvc and how is it used in Spring Boot tests?',options:['A library for mocking HTTP clients','A Spring Test framework component that simulates HTTP requests to a DispatcherServlet without starting a real server, enabling controller testing','An HTTP server that records requests for playback','A Selenium WebDriver wrapper for Spring'],answer:1,explanation:'MockMvc allows testing Spring MVC controllers by performing requests (perform(get("/api/users"))) and asserting responses (andExpect(status().isOk())). It processes the full Spring MVC pipeline (handler mapping, interceptors, response writing) without requiring a real HTTP connection.'},
{id:4948,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @AutoConfigureMockMvc do in Spring Boot test?',options:['Creates a real embedded server','Auto-configures and injects a MockMvc instance into the test class when combined with @SpringBootTest, without needing @WebMvcTest','Configures MockMvc security settings','Is only for reactive applications'],answer:1,explanation:'@AutoConfigureMockMvc, combined with @SpringBootTest, auto-configures MockMvc within the full application context. Unlike @WebMvcTest (which loads only web layer), this approach tests the full stack. The MockMvc bean can then be @Autowired into the test.'},
{id:4949,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between @WebMvcTest and @SpringBootTest + @AutoConfigureMockMvc?',options:['They are identical','@WebMvcTest loads only the web layer (fast, isolated controller tests); @SpringBootTest + @AutoConfigureMockMvc loads the full context (slower, full integration tests)','@WebMvcTest starts a real server; @SpringBootTest does not','@SpringBootTest cannot use MockMvc'],answer:1,explanation:'@WebMvcTest is lightweight: only MVC beans are loaded, services must be @MockBean, making it fast. @SpringBootTest + @AutoConfigureMockMvc loads all beans (services, repositories, etc.) in the full context. Use @WebMvcTest for unit-style controller tests and the latter for full integration tests.'},
{id:4950,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @Testcontainers in the Testcontainers JUnit 5 integration?',options:['A JUnit 5 annotation for running tests inside Docker','A class-level annotation that activates the Testcontainers extension, managing the lifecycle of @Container-annotated fields','A Spring Boot annotation for container testing','A way to skip tests when Docker is unavailable'],answer:1,explanation:'@Testcontainers on a test class activates the Testcontainers JUnit 5 extension. The extension automatically starts and stops containers declared with @Container. Without @Testcontainers, containers must be started/stopped manually in @BeforeAll/@AfterAll.'},
{id:4951,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Container do in Testcontainers with JUnit 5?',options:['Annotates the Docker container image name','Marks a field as a Testcontainers container that the @Testcontainers extension should manage (start before tests, stop after tests)','Declares a Docker Compose file','Specifies a container port mapping'],answer:1,explanation:'@Container on a static field starts the container once for all tests in the class (class-scoped). On an instance field, it starts a fresh container for each test method. The container is stopped automatically after the test scope ends.'},
{id:4952,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Singleton containers pattern in Testcontainers?',options:['Using a single mock instead of a container','Starting a container once for the entire test suite by declaring it in a shared abstract base class with a static @Container field, so all subclasses share it','A pattern where each test class has exactly one container','Running all containers in a single Docker process'],answer:1,explanation:'The Singleton pattern defines a container in a shared base class: static final container field + @Container + @Testcontainers. JUnit 5 + Testcontainers will start the container only once per JVM. All test subclasses share it. Ryuk (Testcontainers cleanup agent) stops it after the JVM exits.'},
{id:4953,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you integrate JUnit 5 with Selenium WebDriver for browser testing?',options:['Use @WebDriver annotation on the test class','Create a JUnit 5 extension that manages WebDriver lifecycle (init in beforeEach, quit in afterEach), or use WebDriverManager with @ExtendWith','Annotate tests with @Browser','Use @SeleniumTest annotation from Selenium JUnit 5 module'],answer:1,explanation:'The recommended approach is a custom extension implementing BeforeEachCallback (WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver()) and AfterEachCallback (driver.quit()). Register it with @ExtendWith. WebDriverManager (io.github.bonigarcia) can handle driver binary downloads and includes JUnit 5 integration.'},
{id:4954,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you integrate JUnit 5 with REST Assured for API testing?',options:['REST Assured requires JUnit 4 and cannot be used with JUnit 5','Use REST Assured normally in @Test methods; it has no JUnit 5-specific extension but works with JUnit 5 tests since it does not depend on the test runner','Use @RestAssuredTest annotation','Add @ExtendWith(RestAssuredExtension.class)'],answer:1,explanation:'REST Assured (io.rest-assured) is runner-agnostic. Use it in @Test methods as normal: given().when().get("/api/users").then().statusCode(200). Configure base URI with RestAssured.baseURI in @BeforeAll. No special JUnit 5 extension is needed.'},
{id:4955,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is InvocationInterceptor in JUnit 5 extensions?',options:['An interface for intercepting method resolution','An extension interface that intercepts calls to test methods, @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, etc., allowing the extension to wrap invocations (e.g., for transaction management or retries)','An interface for intercepting assertion failures','A Spring AOP proxy for test classes'],answer:1,explanation:'InvocationInterceptor provides fine-grained interception of every phase of test execution (interceptTestMethod, interceptBeforeEachMethod, etc.). Each intercept method receives an Invocation<T> and must call invocation.proceed() to continue. Used for transaction wrapping, retry logic, or measuring execution time.'},
{id:4956,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does TestInstancePostProcessor do in JUnit 5?',options:['Runs code after a test instance is created to perform dependency injection or initialization','Creates additional test instances for parallel execution','Modifies the test class bytecode at runtime','Post-processes test results'],answer:0,explanation:'TestInstancePostProcessor.postProcessTestInstance(Object testInstance, ExtensionContext) is called right after a test instance is created but before any test methods run. It enables extensions (like Spring, Mockito) to inject dependencies into the test instance using field injection.'},
{id:4957,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does TestInstancePreDestroyCallback do in JUnit 5?',options:['Prevents test instances from being garbage collected','Called before the test instance is destroyed, enabling cleanup of resources that were set up in TestInstancePostProcessor or test fields','Validates the test instance state','Resets the test instance to initial state'],answer:1,explanation:'TestInstancePreDestroyCallback.preDestroyTestInstance(ExtensionContext) is the counterpart to TestInstancePostProcessor. It is called just before the test instance is destroyed (end of its lifecycle), allowing extensions to release resources tied to the instance.'},
{id:4958,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the ExecutionCondition extension interface used for?',options:['Determines whether a test class should be compiled','Evaluates a condition at runtime to decide whether to execute or skip a test; the basis for @EnabledOnOs, @EnabledIfSystemProperty, etc.','Configures the execution thread for a test','Sets the order of execution for tests'],answer:1,explanation:'ExecutionCondition.evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext) returns a ConditionEvaluationResult that is either enabled or disabled. JUnit 5\'s built-in conditional annotations (@EnabledOnOs, etc.) are all implemented using this interface. Custom conditions can be created by implementing ExecutionCondition.'},
{id:4959,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the ExtensionContext.Store provide in JUnit 5 extensions?',options:['A thread-safe map for storing test results','A key-value store scoped to the extension context (method, class, or root) for sharing state between extension callbacks within the same scope','A database connection pool for tests','A registry for test configuration parameters'],answer:1,explanation:'ExtensionContext.Store is a key-value store. Get it with context.getStore(Namespace.create(MyExtension.class)). Namespacing prevents key collisions between extensions. Store can hold method-scoped, class-scoped, or root-scoped data, accessible across beforeEach/afterEach in the same extension.'},
{id:4960,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does ExtensionContext.Store.CloseableResource work in JUnit 5?',options:['Resources are closed when the JVM shuts down','Values stored in the Store that implement CloseableResource have their close() method called automatically when the associated Store scope (e.g., method, class) ends','Resources must be closed manually in afterEach','CloseableResource is not part of JUnit 5'],answer:1,explanation:'If a stored value implements ExtensionContext.Store.CloseableResource, JUnit 5 automatically calls its close() method when the store\'s scope closes. This enables automatic resource cleanup (e.g., closing a database connection) without requiring an explicit AfterEachCallback in the extension.'},
{id:4961,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure MockMvc to add authentication in @WebMvcTest?',options:['Add credentials to the HTTP headers manually','Use Spring Security Test\'s @WithMockUser on the test method or perform(get("/").with(SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors.user("admin")))','Disable security in the test context','Use @EnableWebSecurity(false) on the test class'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring Security Test provides @WithMockUser to run a test with a mock authenticated user, or with(user("admin").roles("ADMIN")) in MockMvc request builders for per-request authentication. Spring Security auto-configuration is included in @WebMvcTest by default.'},
{id:4962,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of MockMvcResultMatchers in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Verifies database state after controller calls','Provides a fluent DSL for asserting MockMvc response properties: status codes, response body content (JSON/XML), headers, redirects, model attributes, and view names','Records HTTP responses for comparison','Validates request parameters before controller execution'],answer:1,explanation:'MockMvcResultMatchers (status(), content(), jsonPath(), header(), model(), view()) are used with .andExpect() to assert every aspect of the HTTP response. For example, .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Alice")) extracts and verifies a JSON field without parsing the full response manually.'},
{id:4963,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does perform(MockMvcRequestBuilders.post("/api/users").contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON).content(json)) do?',options:['Sends a real HTTP POST to a running server','Simulates an HTTP POST request through MockMvc with JSON body, routing it through the Spring DispatcherServlet without a real network call','Creates a stub for a POST endpoint','Validates the JSON body against a schema'],answer:1,explanation:'This MockMvc call simulates an HTTP POST request. The DispatcherServlet processes it: handler mapping, argument resolution, controller method invocation, response writing—all in-memory. The returned ResultActions can be chained with .andExpect() to assert the response.'},
{id:4964,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @DirtiesContext in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Marks a test as dirty (failing) for CI reports','Indicates that the Spring ApplicationContext is modified by the test and must be closed and recreated after the test method or class','Enables context caching globally','Resets all @MockBean mocks'],answer:1,explanation:'@DirtiesContext forces Spring to close the current application context and create a fresh one. It is expensive but necessary when a test modifies context state (e.g., changes a bean definition). Overuse leads to slow test suites; prefer @MockBean or @TestConfiguration instead.'},
{id:4965,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @ActiveProfiles do in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Activates a Java module','Sets the active Spring profile(s) for the test context, loading profile-specific beans and configuration','Sets the active Maven profile','Enables debug logging for all Spring beans'],answer:1,explanation:'@ActiveProfiles("test") instructs the Spring test context to activate the "test" profile, loading application-test.properties and beans annotated with @Profile("test"). Multiple profiles can be specified: @ActiveProfiles({"test","local"}).'},
{id:4966,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Sql do in Spring Boot JPA tests?',options:['Validates SQL query syntax','Executes the specified SQL script(s) before (or after, with config) the annotated test method or class, useful for setting up database state','Generates SQL from entity annotations','Runs EXPLAIN ANALYZE on test queries'],answer:1,explanation:'@Sql("/test-data.sql") runs the SQL file before the test. @SqlConfig and executionPhase can control execution timing. Combined with @DataJpaTest, it pre-populates the in-memory database with test data without needing @BeforeEach Java code.'},
{id:4967,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does Spring Boot test context caching work in JUnit 5?',options:['Each @SpringBootTest creates a new context','Spring caches ApplicationContexts keyed by their configuration; tests with identical configurations share the same context, drastically reducing startup time across the test suite','Context is cached per test method only','Context caching requires explicit @CacheContext annotation'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring\'s test context framework caches loaded ApplicationContexts in a static map keyed by context configuration attributes. Tests sharing the same @SpringBootTest config, @ActiveProfiles, and @MockBean set reuse the cached context. @DirtiesContext or @MockBean changes break the cache.'},
{id:4968,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @TestPropertySource do in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Overrides the application properties file entirely','Inlines property values or specifies property file locations that override the application context properties for the test','Generates random property values','Reads properties from environment variables only'],answer:1,explanation:'@TestPropertySource(properties={"server.port=8081"}) or @TestPropertySource(locations="/test.properties") overrides specific properties for the test context. Inline properties take precedence over file locations. Useful for overriding feature flags or external service URLs in tests.'},
{id:4969,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you verify interactions with a @MockBean in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Use assertTrue on the result','Use Mockito\'s verify() method: verify(mockBean, times(1)).someMethod(args)','Use @VerifyBean annotation','Check the bean\'s return value'],answer:1,explanation:'@MockBean creates a Mockito mock registered in the context. Standard Mockito verification works: verify(myMockBean).doSomething(expectedArg). Mockito.verify is the same regardless of whether the mock was created with @Mock or @MockBean.'},
{id:4970,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @JsonTest in Spring Boot?',options:['Tests JSON schema validation','Loads only Jackson auto-configuration and provides a JacksonTester for testing JSON serialization and deserialization of domain objects','Tests REST endpoints that produce JSON','Validates JSON path expressions'],answer:1,explanation:'@JsonTest sets up only the JSON-related slice: Jackson ObjectMapper, @JsonComponent beans. It provides @Autowired JacksonTester<MyClass> for asserting serialized JSON: assertThat(json.write(obj)).extractingJsonPathStringValue("$.name").isEqualTo("Alice").'},
{id:4971,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is @RestClientTest in Spring Boot testing?',options:['Tests RestController endpoints','Tests client-side REST code using a MockRestServiceServer that stubs HTTP responses, loading only the beans needed for RestTemplate/RestClient configuration','Starts a real test server for REST client tests','Tests WebClient reactive REST clients'],answer:1,explanation:'@RestClientTest(MyService.class) sets up a minimal context with MockRestServiceServer. The server stubs HTTP responses without a real network. Test the service\'s RestTemplate/RestClient calls by expecting requests and defining responses: mockServer.expect(requestTo("/api")).andRespond(withSuccess(body, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)).'},
{id:4972,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does Testcontainers\' @Container on an instance field (not static) do?',options:['Starts the container once for all tests in the class','Starts and stops a fresh container for each individual test method','Starts the container lazily on first use','Shares the container across all test classes'],answer:1,explanation:'An instance field annotated with @Container is started before each test method and stopped after it, providing complete isolation. A static field starts once for the class. Use instance-scoped containers when tests might pollute the container state.'},
{id:4973,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you map dynamic Testcontainer ports to Spring application properties?',options:['Hard-code the port in application-test.properties','Use @DynamicPropertySource: a static method annotated with @DynamicPropertySource receives a DynamicPropertyRegistry to register container.getFirstMappedPort() as a Spring property','Use @TestPropertySource with a placeholder','Configure it in @BeforeAll using System.setProperty'],answer:1,explanation:'@DynamicPropertySource (Spring 5.2+) provides a clean way to register dynamically allocated container ports. The static method receives a DynamicPropertyRegistry: registry.add("spring.datasource.url", () -> container.getJdbcUrl()). Spring uses these values when building the application context.'},
{id:4974,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What Maven artifact provides Testcontainers JUnit 5 integration?',options:['org.testcontainers:testcontainers-junit5','org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter','com.testcontainers:junit5','org.junit.testcontainers:core'],answer:1,explanation:'The artifact org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter provides the @Testcontainers and @Container annotations and the Testcontainers JUnit 5 extension. It must be on the test classpath alongside the specific container module (e.g., org.testcontainers:postgresql).'},
{id:4975,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the WireMock JUnit 5 extension and how is it registered?',options:['WireMock requires a separate JUnit 4 runner','Use @WireMockTest (from com.github.tomakehurst:wiremock) as a class annotation, or register WireMockExtension via @RegisterExtension to configure it with programmatic options','Use @ExtendWith(WireMock.class)','WireMock only works with Spring Boot Test'],answer:1,explanation:'@WireMockTest (WireMock 3.x) is a convenience annotation that starts a WireMock server on a random port for each test. For more control, use @RegisterExtension with WireMockExtension.newInstance().options(wireMockConfig().port(8089)).build(). Both register the JUnit 5 extension.'},
{id:4976,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does Awaitility provide in JUnit 5 testing?',options:['A mechanism to await Spring context startup','A DSL for testing asynchronous code by polling a condition until it is satisfied or a timeout expires, without blocking threads','A way to pause tests for a fixed duration','An async assertion that retries assertions in parallel'],answer:1,explanation:'Awaitility.await().atMost(5, SECONDS).until(() -> myService.isDone()) polls the condition repeatedly until it returns true or the timeout expires. This is superior to Thread.sleep for testing async behavior since it reacts immediately when the condition is met.'},
{id:4977,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you test an asynchronous CompletableFuture in JUnit 5?',options:['Just call .get() and assert the result','Use assertTimeout with the future\'s get()', 'Either call future.get() with a timeout inside assertDoesNotThrow, or use Awaitility to poll a side effect', 'Use @Async test annotation'],answer:2,explanation:'For CompletableFuture, calling future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS) within assertDoesNotThrow or assertTimeout works for simple cases. For side-effect-based async code, Awaitility is more flexible. assertTimeoutPreemptively can also wrap async operations to enforce strict time limits.'},
{id:4978,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the @DisabledIfSystemProperty annotation in JUnit 5?',options:['Is deprecated; use @EnabledIfSystemProperty instead','Disables the test when the specified system property matches the given regex value','Removes a system property before the test runs','Fails the test if the property is set'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledIfSystemProperty(named="skipTests", matches="true") disables the test if the JVM system property "skipTests" equals "true" (regex). It is the inverse of @EnabledIfSystemProperty and allows tests to be conditionally skipped via external configuration without modifying test code.'},
{id:4979,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @DisabledIfEnvironmentVariable in JUnit 5?',options:['Fails the test if the variable is not set','Disables the test if the specified environment variable matches the given regex','Sets the variable to an empty string','Replaces the variable with a mock value'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named="CI", matches="true") disables the test in a CI environment where the CI environment variable is set to "true". Useful for excluding tests that require manual interaction or specific hardware not available in CI.'},
{id:4980,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you create a composed conditional annotation in JUnit 5?',options:['Extend a ConditionalAnnotation base class','Create an annotation meta-annotated with @EnabledOnOs, @EnabledOnJre, or a custom @ExtendWith(ExecutionCondition), so applying the composed annotation applies all conditions','Use @AggregateCondition','Conditions cannot be composed'],answer:1,explanation:'Create a custom annotation: @Target(METHOD) @Retention(RUNTIME) @EnabledOnOs(OS.LINUX) @EnabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_17). Any test annotated with @LinuxJava17Only will be enabled only on Linux with Java 17. JUnit 5\'s meta-annotation support reads all composed conditions.'},
{id:4981,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledInNativeImage do in JUnit 5?',options:['Disables tests when running in a GraalVM native image','Disables tests compiled with native compilers','Disables tests with native dependencies','Marks tests that cannot be run in parallel'],answer:0,explanation:'@DisabledInNativeImage (added in JUnit 5.10) disables a test when executing as a GraalVM native image. Some tests (e.g., those using reflection-based features) cannot run in native images and should be excluded rather than fail.'},
{id:4982,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @EnabledInNativeImage in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables tests only in native image builds; disables them in standard JVM execution','Forces compilation to native','Marks tests as native-image compatible','Enables GraalVM-specific assertions'],answer:0,explanation:'@EnabledInNativeImage enables a test only when running as a GraalVM native image. This can be used for tests that specifically test native-image behavior and should not run in normal JVM execution, providing the complement to @DisabledInNativeImage.'},
{id:4983,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you use ConditionEvaluationResult in a custom ExecutionCondition?',options:['Return true or false from the evaluateExecutionCondition method','Return ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled("reason") to allow or ConditionEvaluationResult.disabled("reason") to skip the test; the reason is shown in reports','Throw an exception to disable; return null to enable','Set a static flag on the JUnit context'],answer:1,explanation:'A custom ExecutionCondition overrides evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext) and returns ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled(reason) or ConditionEvaluationResult.disabled(reason). The reason string appears in test reports, making it clear why tests were conditionally skipped.'},
{id:4984,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT) do?',options:['Starts the app on port 8080','Starts the embedded server on a randomly available port; inject the port with @LocalServerPort','Disables the web context','Uses a mock web environment'],answer:1,explanation:'RANDOM_PORT starts the full embedded server on an available port. @LocalServerPort (or @Value("${local.server.port}")) injects the actual port. This enables real HTTP testing with RestTemplate or WebTestClient against a fully running application.'},
{id:4985,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is WebTestClient in Spring Boot testing?',options:['A MockMvc wrapper for reactive apps','A non-blocking, fluent test client for testing Spring WebFlux and Spring MVC controllers, supporting both real and mock server scenarios','A wrapper for REST Assured','A browser-based testing tool'],answer:1,explanation:'WebTestClient (spring-test) provides a fluent API for testing reactive and servlet-based web endpoints: client.get().uri("/api/users").exchange().expectStatus().isOk().expectBody(User.class). It works with @SpringBootTest (real server) or can be wired directly to a WebFlux/MVC handler without a server.'},
{id:4986,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happens if multiple @ExtendWith extensions are registered and both implement BeforeEachCallback?',options:['Only the first extension runs','Both run; the order is determined by the order of @ExtendWith declarations (outer to inner), following the "wrapping" semantics','The last declared extension takes precedence','A conflict error is thrown'],answer:1,explanation:'Multiple extensions are applied in the order they are declared in @ExtendWith. BeforeEachCallback runs in outer-to-inner (declaration) order. AfterEachCallback runs in the reverse order. This creates a "wrapping" or decorator effect, similar to nested try-finally blocks.'},
{id:4987,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the ExtensionContext hierarchy in JUnit 5?',options:['A flat list of all test methods','A tree of contexts: root > engine > class > method; each level is accessible via context.getParent() and provides scoped stores and metadata','A parallel execution graph','A dependency graph of extensions'],answer:1,explanation:'ExtensionContext forms a hierarchy. The root context encompasses the entire test run. Engine contexts are per TestEngine. Class contexts are per test class. Method contexts are per test method. Extensions can access parent contexts to share data at higher levels via getParent().get().getStore().'},
{id:4988,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @TestMethodOrder(MethodOrderer.MethodName.class) do in JUnit 5.8+?',options:['Orders methods by annotation name','Orders test methods alphanumerically based on the method name, replacing the deprecated Alphanumeric orderer','Orders methods by return type','Orders methods by parameter count'],answer:1,explanation:'MethodOrderer.MethodName (added in JUnit 5.8) orders test methods alphanumerically by method name. It replaces the deprecated MethodOrderer.Alphanumeric, which considered parameter type names. MethodName ordering considers only the method name itself.'},
{id:4989,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @TempDir\'s cleanup strategy if a test fails?',options:['The temp directory is kept for debugging','The temp directory is still deleted after the test even if the test fails','Cleanup only happens if the test passes','Cleanup requires @AfterEach'],answer:1,explanation:'@TempDir always cleans up the temporary directory after the test scope ends, regardless of whether the test passed or failed. If cleanup fails (e.g., a file is locked), a warning is logged but the test result is not affected. Cleanup mode can be configured with @TempDir(cleanup=NEVER) in JUnit 5.9+.'},
{id:4990,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @TempDir(cleanup = CleanupMode.NEVER) do in JUnit 5.9+?',options:['Disables the TempDir feature entirely','Creates the temporary directory but never deletes it, useful for post-failure inspection of test artifacts','Schedules cleanup for the next JVM restart','Throws an exception if the directory is not empty'],answer:1,explanation:'CleanupMode.NEVER tells JUnit not to delete the temp directory after the test. This is useful for debugging: inspect generated files after a test failure. CleanupMode.ON_SUCCESS only cleans up after passing tests. CleanupMode.ALWAYS (default) always cleans up.'},
{id:4991,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does @ParameterizedTest display names work with @CsvSource in JUnit 5?',options:['Each invocation gets the method name plus index','By default the display name includes the index and argument values; a custom pattern can be set via the name attribute, e.g., name="[{index}] name={0}, age={1}"','Display names are not supported for CSV sources','Only the row number is shown'],answer:1,explanation:'@ParameterizedTest(name="[{index}] {0} is {1} years old") formats the display name using the declared placeholders. JUnit replaces {index} with the invocation number, {0} with the first argument, {1} with the second. This creates readable test entries in IDE and build reports.'},
{id:4992,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @NullSource in conjunction with other parameterized sources?',options:['@NullSource cannot be combined with other sources','Stacking @NullSource with @ValueSource or @CsvSource adds a null invocation to the existing argument set','@NullSource replaces all other sources','@NullSource only works with String parameters'],answer:1,explanation:'Stacking source annotations (e.g., @NullSource + @ValueSource(strings={"Alice","Bob"})) combines them: the test runs with null, then "Alice", then "Bob". This pattern is idiomatic for testing methods that must handle both null and non-null inputs.'},
{id:4993,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the role of the JUnit 5 @SuiteDisplayName annotation?',options:['Sets the display name for a test method suite','Sets the display name for a @Suite class as shown in test reports and IDEs','Is equivalent to @DisplayName','Configures the suite file name for reporting tools'],answer:1,explanation:'@SuiteDisplayName("My Integration Suite") on a @Suite class sets the human-readable name shown in reports and IDEs. Without it, the class name is used. It is the suite-level equivalent of the method/class-level @DisplayName annotation.'},
{id:4994,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @IncludeClassNamePatterns in a JUnit 5 @Suite?',options:['Filters which class names are shown in reports','Specifies regex patterns; only classes whose fully qualified names match a pattern are included in the suite','Restricts the package scan depth','Filters class names by inheritance'],answer:1,explanation:'@IncludeClassNamePatterns(".*IntegrationTest") on a @Suite includes only classes whose fully qualified name matches the regex. Paired with @SelectPackages, it provides fine-grained control over which test classes are selected without listing each explicitly.'},
{id:4995,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @ExcludeClassNamePatterns in a JUnit 5 @Suite?',options:['Excludes class names from reports','Specifies regex patterns; classes whose fully qualified names match are excluded from the suite','Hides classes from the IDE','Excludes abstract base classes automatically'],answer:1,explanation:'@ExcludeClassNamePatterns(".*SlowTest") on a @Suite excludes matching test classes. Combined with @IncludeClassNamePatterns, you can precisely define which classes run, complementing @IncludeTags/@ExcludeTags for tag-based filtering.'},
{id:4996,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @IncludeEngines do in a JUnit 5 @Suite?',options:['Lists the Java engines to compile with','Specifies which TestEngines (by ID) should be included in the suite execution, e.g., "junit-jupiter" or "junit-vintage"','Enables additional assertion engines','Configures which assertion library to use'],answer:1,explanation:'@IncludeEngines("junit-jupiter") restricts the suite to only run tests discovered by the JUnit Jupiter engine. Using @IncludeEngines("junit-vintage") would run only JUnit 4 tests. This is useful in mixed projects to run only a specific generation of tests.'},
{id:4997,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @TestInstancePreConstructCallback do in JUnit 5?',options:['Prevents test instance creation if a condition fails','Called before the test instance constructor is invoked, enabling pre-construction setup in extensions','Sets constructor parameters for the test class','Validates the test class structure before instantiation'],answer:1,explanation:'TestInstancePreConstructCallback.preConstructTestInstance(TestInstanceFactoryContext, ExtensionContext) is called before the test class constructor runs. It is useful for extensions that need to set up thread locals or other pre-construction state that the constructor may depend on.'},
{id:4998,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is a TestInstanceFactory in JUnit 5?',options:['A factory class for creating mock instances','An extension interface that takes full control of test class instantiation, replacing JUnit\'s default constructor-based instantiation','A factory for creating parameterized test instances','A Spring bean factory for test classes'],answer:1,explanation:'TestInstanceFactory.createTestInstance(TestInstanceFactoryContext, ExtensionContext) allows an extension to control how test instances are created. Only one TestInstanceFactory can be registered per class. Spring\'s SpringExtension uses this to create test instances via the Spring context (e.g., for @Autowired fields).'},
{id:4999,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does JUnit 5 handle extensions that throw exceptions in BeforeAllCallback?',options:['The exception is swallowed and tests run normally','The exception causes all tests in the container to fail with the extension exception, and AfterAllCallback is still invoked if registered','Only the first test fails','The test class is skipped without failure'],answer:1,explanation:'If BeforeAllCallback throws an exception, all tests in the container are reported as failed with the extension exception as the cause. JUnit 5 still invokes AfterAllCallback to allow cleanup. This is analogous to an exception in @BeforeAll causing test failures.'},
{id:5000,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledOnJre do in JUnit 5?',options:['Disables the JRE for tests','Prevents the test from running on specified JRE versions (e.g., @DisabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_8))','Downgrades the JRE during testing','Marks the test as JRE version independent'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_8) skips the test when running on Java 8. It is the inverse of @EnabledOnJre and useful for excluding tests that rely on APIs not available (or different) in specific JRE versions.'},
{id:5001,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How does @MockBean interact with Spring context caching?',options:['@MockBean has no impact on caching','Each unique set of @MockBean declarations is treated as part of the context configuration key, so tests with different @MockBean sets load separate contexts','@MockBean always disables context caching','All @MockBean tests share one context'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring context caching considers @MockBean declarations as part of the cache key. Tests that share the same application context config AND the same @MockBean types can reuse the cached context. Adding a new @MockBean to a test forces a new context to be created, increasing startup time.'},
{id:5002,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @BootstrapWith in Spring Boot tests?',options:['Bootstraps a database schema','Specifies a custom TestContextBootstrapper class that controls how the Spring TestContext is built','Initializes the Spring security layer','Starts the embedded server'],answer:1,explanation:'@BootstrapWith(SpringBootTestContextBootstrapper.class) is used internally by @SpringBootTest. Custom bootstrappers can override context building, property resolution, and bean factory initialization strategies. It is rarely needed directly but useful for framework-level customization.'},
{id:5003,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @RecordFileOutput do in JUnit 5?',options:['Annotates a @TempDir field to save generated files permanently','Is not a standard JUnit 5 annotation; it refers to a community extension or custom implementation for capturing file output from tests','Records screen output to a file','Marks fields whose file content is compared post-test'],answer:1,explanation:'@RecordFileOutput is not a standard JUnit 5 annotation. It may refer to community extensions or custom implementations that capture a test\'s file or console output for comparison or reporting. Always verify the artifact providing this annotation; it is not in the core JUnit 5 library.'},
{id:5004,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is JUnit 5\'s support for @MethodSource with multiple parameters?',options:['Multiple parameters are not supported; use a single String[]','Return Stream<Arguments> where each Arguments.of(arg1, arg2, ...) entry provides values for multiple parameters, which JUnit maps positionally to the method signature','Multiple parameters require @CsvSource','Use a List<Object[]> instead of Stream<Arguments>'],answer:1,explanation:'A @MethodSource factory returning Stream.of(Arguments.of("Alice", 30), Arguments.of("Bob", 25)) maps values positionally to the test method parameters (String name, int age). JUnit uses implicit type conversion for each argument individually, same as for CSV sources.'},
{id:5005,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @TestMethodOrder with MethodOrderer.Random use as a seed by default?',options:['System.nanoTime() on each run','A random seed that changes every run; set junit.jupiter.execution.order.random.seed=42 for reproducibility','The hash of the test class name','The current date'],answer:0,explanation:'MethodOrderer.Random generates a new random seed on each run to maximize randomness and help expose order dependencies. For reproducible ordering (e.g., reproducing a CI failure), set junit.jupiter.execution.order.random.seed to a specific value in junit-platform.properties or as a system property.'},
{id:5006,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you use @ArgumentsSource with a custom ArgumentsProvider that needs the test annotation?',options:['Override annotationProcessor() method','Implement AnnotationConsumer<MyAnnotation> in the ArgumentsProvider; JUnit injects the annotation before provideArguments() is called','Pass the annotation class to the @ArgumentsSource','Access annotations via ExtensionContext'],answer:1,explanation:'Implementing both ArgumentsProvider and AnnotationConsumer<T> in the provider class causes JUnit 5 to call accept(T annotation) before provideArguments(). This allows the provider to read configuration from a custom annotation applied to the test method.'},
{id:5007,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a @ParameterizedTest implicit argument conversion and when does it fail?',options:['Conversion always succeeds','JUnit 5 automatically converts String values to target types using valueOf(), from() factory methods, or single-String constructors; it fails with ParameterConversionException when no converter exists','Conversion only works for primitives','Conversion fails silently and injects null'],answer:1,explanation:'JUnit\'s implicit conversion (TypeConvertingArgumentConverter) tries valueOf(String), from(String), fromValue(String), or a single-String constructor. If none are found, a ParameterConversionException is thrown at runtime. For custom types, use @ConvertWith to specify an explicit ArgumentConverter.'},
{id:5008,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does MockMvc\'s andDo(print()) do?',options:['Prints the test report to file','Prints the full MVC request and response details to the console/log for debugging','Prints the test name before execution','Logs assertion results'],answer:1,explanation:'andDo(MockMvcResultHandlers.print()) outputs a detailed dump of the request (method, URI, headers, body) and response (status, headers, body) to System.out or a configured output stream. Invaluable for debugging failing controller tests without running a live server.'},
{id:5009,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In @WebMvcTest, how do you stub a service method called by the controller?',options:['@Autowired the real service and call methods on it','Use @MockBean to register a mock of the service in the context, then stub with Mockito: when(mockService.findUser(1L)).thenReturn(user)','Use @Spy on the service','Override the service method in the test class'],answer:1,explanation:'In @WebMvcTest, service layer beans are not loaded. Declare the service as @MockBean to register a mock in the context. The controller receives this mock via Spring injection. Stub with Mockito\'s when().thenReturn() before performing the MockMvc request.'},
{id:5010,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does REST Assured\'s given().baseUri().when().get().then().statusCode() pattern represent?',options:['A Builder pattern for HTTP request construction','The BDD-style fluent API of REST Assured: given() sets up request specs, when() performs the action (HTTP call), then() defines response assertions','A reactive stream pipeline','A JUnit 5 extension chain'],answer:1,explanation:'REST Assured uses a given-when-then BDD pattern: given() configures headers/params/body, when().get("/endpoint") sends the request, then().statusCode(200).body("name", equalTo("Alice")) asserts the response. This produces readable, self-documenting API test code.'},
{id:5011,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you run REST Assured tests against a Spring Boot test server started with @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT)?',options:['Hard-code port 8080','Inject the port with @LocalServerPort and set RestAssured.port = port in @BeforeAll, or configure a RequestSpecification with the base URI','Use RestAssured.baseURI = "http://localhost"','REST Assured does not work with random ports'],answer:1,explanation:'Inject @LocalServerPort int port in the test class. In @BeforeAll, set RestAssured.port = port (or create a RequestSpecification with the base URI). REST Assured then targets the randomly assigned port, enabling real end-to-end HTTP tests without port conflicts.'},
{id:5012,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a TestExecutionListener and how does it differ from a JUnit 5 Extension?',options:['They are identical','TestExecutionListener is a JUnit Platform API for tools (IDEs, build plugins, reporters) to observe test execution from the outside; Extension is a Jupiter API that participates in the test lifecycle from within the Jupiter engine','TestExecutionListener runs before the extension','Extension is a build tool concept; TestExecutionListener is for test code'],answer:1,explanation:'Extensions are Jupiter-specific and hook into the test lifecycle at the engine level: parameter injection, condition evaluation, callbacks. TestExecutionListeners are Platform-level observers that receive high-level events (testStarted, testFinished) after they happen, used by reporters and CI integrations rather than test code.'},
{id:5013,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does Mockito\'s strict stubbing mode do with JUnit 5?',options:['Allows unused stubs silently','Fails the test if a stub is set up but never called (unnecessary stubbing), or if stubbed args differ from actual invocation args, promoting cleaner tests','Prevents all stubbing of methods','Enforces alphabetical ordering of stubs'],answer:1,explanation:'MockitoExtension uses STRICT_STUBS mode by default, which detects unnecessary stubbing (stubbings never invoked) and incorrect argument stubbing. Tests fail with a UnnecessaryStubbingException or PotentialStubbingProblem, preventing leftover stubs from masking intent.'},
{id:5014,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you capture multiple invocations with ArgumentCaptor in Mockito?',options:['Call captor.getValue() multiple times','Call verify(mock, times(n)).method(captor.capture()) and then use captor.getAllValues() to get a list of all captured arguments from each invocation','Create a new captor for each invocation','Use captureAll() method'],answer:1,explanation:'After verify(mock, times(3)).method(captor.capture()), calling captor.getAllValues() returns a List<T> with all three captured arguments in invocation order. captor.getValue() returns only the most recently captured argument. getAllValues() is essential for multi-invocation argument verification.'},
{id:5015,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is Mockito\'s @Captor and how does MockitoExtension initialize it?',options:['@Captor must be initialized manually with ArgumentCaptor.forClass()','MockitoExtension calls MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this) (or equivalent) in BeforeEachCallback, which initializes @Captor fields using reflection, creating typed ArgumentCaptors automatically','@Captor is initialized by Spring','@Captor fields are static and initialized at class load time'],answer:1,explanation:'@Captor on an ArgumentCaptor<Type> field is initialized by MockitoExtension\'s BeforeEachCallback, which processes all Mockito annotations via MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(). This avoids the verbose ArgumentCaptor.forClass(Type.class) syntax and ensures the captor is reset before each test.'},
{id:5016,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the difference between @Mock and @Spy in terms of default method behavior?',options:['They are identical','@Mock returns default values (null/0/false) for all methods; @Spy calls the real method unless specifically stubbed','@Spy returns null for all methods; @Mock calls the real method','@Mock and @Spy differ only in how they record interactions'],answer:1,explanation:'@Mock creates a full mock: all methods return default values and do not call real code. @Spy wraps a real object: non-stubbed methods call the actual implementation and return real results. Spy is useful when only specific methods need to be replaced in an otherwise real implementation.'},
{id:5017,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happens if @InjectMocks cannot find a suitable constructor or setter for injection?',options:['A NullPointerException is thrown','Mockito falls back to field injection; if no field matches by type, the dependency is left uninjected (null), and Mockito does not throw an error','Mockito throws an InjectionFailureException','The test is skipped'],answer:1,explanation:'Mockito\'s injection strategy: constructor injection → setter injection → field injection. If none succeed for a particular dependency, the field remains null. Mockito does not fail the test; you must add an assertion to detect missing injection. This silent failure is a common source of NullPointerExceptions in tests.'},
{id:5018,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does MockitoExtension handle @BeforeEach interactions with Mockito mocks?',options:['Mocks are created anew in @BeforeEach automatically','MockitoExtension creates mocks before @BeforeEach runs (via BeforeEachCallback), so mocks are available and can be used in @BeforeEach for stubbing or verification','Mocks are created after @BeforeEach','Mocks cannot be used in @BeforeEach'],answer:1,explanation:'MockitoExtension implements BeforeEachCallback and runs before JUnit\'s @BeforeEach lifecycle methods. This ensures that all @Mock, @Spy, and @Captor fields are initialized before @BeforeEach executes, so @BeforeEach can safely stub or configure mocks.'},
{id:5019,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does verify(mock, never()).method() assert in Mockito?',options:['That the method was called exactly once with no arguments','That the method was never called on the mock during the test','That the method always throws an exception','That no stubs were set up for the method'],answer:1,explanation:'verify(mock, never()).method() fails if the method was called at least once. It is equivalent to verify(mock, times(0)).method(). Used to assert that a code path is NOT taken, e.g., verifying that a notification service was not called when a precondition was not met.'},
{id:5020,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is Mockito\'s verifyNoMoreInteractions() and when should it be used?',options:['Verifies that no stubs were created','Asserts that all interactions with the given mocks have been verified; fails if any unverified interactions remain','Clears all mock invocation history','Validates that no exceptions were thrown from mocks'],answer:1,explanation:'verifyNoMoreInteractions(mock) fails if any method call on the mock was not explicitly verified with verify(). Use it to assert that the system under test does not make unexpected calls. Use judiciously—it makes tests brittle to refactoring; prefer verifying only what matters.'},
]);
